  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nIf you were to look up \"boring\" in the dictionary, you might see a \nsnapshot of our current synoptic pattern. Amplified mid-level \nridging persists over much of the eastern third of the CONUS, which \nhas been gradually battered down by impulses from the north and west \nover the past couple of days. Broad troughing over the Intermountain \nWest is projected to continue this trend, further pushing the ridge \naxis off towards the east with its coincident surface high sliding \noff towards the Ohio River Valley as it traverses the upper flow. \nEven with this shift in the pattern oncoming, deep \nsouth/southwesterly winds will continue to contribute to daytime \nhighs in the high 70s for today. \n\nWith the adjustment of the ridge axis off towards the east, the \nplacement of the forecast area under the periphery of the ridge will \nopen us up to greater opportunities for precipitation in the coming \ndays. By Monday evening into Tuesday morning, a shortwave will lift \nover the Central Plains along the periphery of the ridge, and bring \nwith it a narrow corridor of moisture. This will work to moisten a \nrelatively dry vertical profile, and bring chances (15-30%) of rain. \nSynoptic ascent doesn't look overly impressive during this \ntimeframe, with the primary shortwave having lifted off further \nnortheast by the time precipitation begins across the area. However, \ninterrogating guidance, it looks like convergence with a quasi-\nstationary boundary draped behind the lifting surface low collocated \nwith a narrow corridor of vorticity/weak dCVA aloft and weak flow \nover the boundary in the low-levels will contribute to precipitation \nchances Tuesday morning. With the location of the boundary remaining \njust to the northwest of the forecast area, greatest precipitation \nchances will reside across far eastern KS/western MO during the \nearly half of Tuesday. Greater cloud debris and precipitation \npotential will mitigate high temperatures for Tuesday, bringing them \ncloser to average at around the low/mid 70s. Another opportunity for \nprecipitation will come late Tuesday night, with a more vigorous \nshortwave lifting from the Southern Plains across central MO. \nPrecipitation potential looks to be primarily driven by synoptic \nascent from dCVA and the LLJ tapping into residual moisture in the \nlow to mid-levels, keeping chances (15-30%) around through Wednesday \nmorning. An enhanced pressure gradient that develops with a strong \nsurface high over the northeastern CONUS and a weak cyclone \ndeveloping off the lee of the Rockies will bring southerly winds up \non Wednesday to ~15-20 mph. With gradual clearing of precipitation, \ntemperatures will rise once again to the mid/high 70s during the day \non Wednesday.\n\nBy Wednesday evening, yet another opportunity for precipitation \narises with a cold frontal boundary draping down across the north-\ncentral CONUS. Continued south/southwesterly flow will allow some \ninstability to build out ahead of the front, with shear/lapse rates \nremaining just marginal enough to lower confidence on greater severe \npotential with any activity that develops. However, with this kind \nof signal at this time of year, future forecast iterations will need \nto monitor trends to see how confidence shifts with severe \npotential. Precipitation chances remain confined to the location of \nthe boundary through the night, with northern MO seeing the highest \nchances (50-70%) of precipitation Wednesday evening. Guidance \nremains in pretty good agreement for precipitation chances \ncontinuing through the latter half of the week with continued \ndisturbances in the upper-level flow, which isn't great news for \nthose who prefer treats over tricks... Something to look forward to \nis temperatures cooling off by the end of the forecast period, with \nstrong warm flow cut off to the south and greater east/southeasterly \nflow. However, at this point in the forecast, greater deviations in \nguidance arise with the synoptic pattern. So, as much as cooler \ntemperatures sound great right about now, trends will continue to be \nmonitored in the long term to see if this solution becomes more \napparent.",
